<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Amphilochian Argos</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Argos Amphilochikon</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Amphilochian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argo">Argos</a> was an ancient Greek city at the south-eastern corner of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ambracian-gulf">Ambracian Gulf</a>, in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>, the chief town of the Amphilochians. Legend named the hero Amphilochus as its founder. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peloponnesian-war">Peloponnesian War</a> it was attacked by Ambracia, and in 426 BCE the Athenian general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/demosthenes-general">Demosthenes</a> and the Acarnanians defeated the Ambraciots nearby at Olpae and Idomene.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Amphilochian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argo">Argos</a> stood at the eastern end of the Ambraciot Gulf on the river Inachus, and its territory was called Argeia. Its people claimed descent from colonists of Argos in Peloponnesus. One tradition held that Amphilochus, son of Amphiaraüs, unhappy with affairs in Argos on his return from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trojan-war">Trojan War</a>, founded a city of the same name; another said that Alcmaeon founded it and named it after his brother Amphilochus.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>According to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thucydides">Thucydides</a>, the people of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argo">Argos</a> were the only Amphilochians to have taken up Greek speech, which they owed to settlers from Ambracia whom they had admitted. The Ambraciots later drove out the original inhabitants, who turned to the Acarnanians and then to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>. An Athenian force under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phormio">Phormio</a> took the town, sold the Ambraciots as slaves and restored it to the Amphilochians and Acarnanians, probably in 432 BCE. The Ambraciots ravaged its land in 430 BCE but could not take it.</p><p>In 426 BCE they tried again with the Spartan commander Eurylochus, seizing the hill of Olpae. Demosthenes, commanding the Acarnanians, hid men in a dell and won a decisive victory in which Eurylochus was killed, then surprised an Ambraciot relief force asleep at Idomene. Thucydides judged it the heaviest loss any Greek city suffered in the war before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peace-of-nicias">Peace of Nicias</a>. The Acarnanians and Amphilochians then made a hundred-year peace with Ambracia.</p><p>After the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a> the town passed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aetolians">Aetolians</a>, and the Roman general M. Fulvius stayed there when he concluded the treaty between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> and the Aetolians. After the Battle of Actium Augustus moved its people to his new city of Nicopolis, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argo">Argos</a> was deserted.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The site is disputed. In the 19th century William Martin Leake placed it at Neokhori in the plain of Vlikha, where an ancient wall about a mile around survives, and the editors of the Barrington Atlas of the Greek and Roman World tentatively identifies it with Ag. Ioannes, near Neokhori.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Amphilochian Argos” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the Peloponnesian War it fought the Acarnanians and <b>Amphilochian Argos</b>, and in 426 BCE the Athenian general Demosthenes destroyed its army at Idomene.</blockquote><a href="/state/ambracia">Ambracia</a></li></ul></section>
